From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:18:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(design): DESIGN-03 - /design was offering two floors we
 do not lay, and one of them was mine
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The catalogue has always known which combinations are not products.
/floor-studio has always asked it. This page never did, and DESIGN-01 made that
matter by adding the board-width control without wiring it to the rules:

  · FUMED & SMOKED ON WALNUT, MAPLE OR HICKORY. Fuming is an ammonia reaction
    with the tannin in the wood and those three do not carry enough of it. The
    catalogue says so, with that reason written out. /design offered it anyway,
    on every species, and would have priced it and put it on a spec sheet.
    Pre-existing.

  · HERRINGBONE OR CHEVRON AT 7 INCHES OR WIDER. Both are cut as blocks, and
    past five inches the proportion stops reading as a pattern; the catalogue
    says we lay them at 3 1/4 and 5. This one is mine. I added the width row
    and never asked. Worse: I rendered hickory herringbone at 8-plus as one of
    the six sample plates in the DESIGN-01 handover and offered it as evidence
    the feature worked. It was a picture of a floor this company does not lay.

Every control on /design now moves through withAxis, which the studio already
uses: it changes the axis the visitor pressed and repairs whichever other one
broke, never the one they touched, and narrates what moved. Options that are
not products are dimmed and carry the reason in their title, and stay
pressable — because pressing one is not a mistake to be blocked, it is a
request to be honoured by moving something else. incompatibilities returns
reasons rather than a boolean precisely so the UI can say "hard maple has no
tannin for the ammonia to react with", which teaches somebody something true
about wood. A control that greys itself out only teaches them the site is
broken.

AND THE EXHAUSTIVE CHECK FOUND A THIRD ONE, IN THE CATALOGUE, AFFECTING BOTH PAGES

withAxis guarded the finish repair with `axis !== 'finishId'` — correctly, the
pressed axis must stand — and nothing took its place. So pressing "Fumed &
Smoked" while on maple satisfied no repair branch, failed the final isLayable,
and fell through to the DEFAULT_CONFIGURATION fallback: it threw away the
visitor's species, pattern AND width, returned white oak / satin / straight /
5in, and reported `repairedAxes: []` while doing it. That list is what the UI
builds its "we moved the ..." sentence from, so the visitor was told nothing at
all. /floor-studio has been doing this the whole time.

The missing branch is the one the rule already implies: they pressed the
FINISH, so the SPECIES is what moves. Somebody asking for a fumed floor wants a
fumed floor, and oak is the wood that takes it. Pressing smoked on maple now
gives white oak smoked chevron 5in — species moved, pattern and width kept,
and the sentence says the species moved. The last-resort fallback also names
what it took instead of claiming it took nothing.

The test that should have caught this looped ONE axis from ONE starting
configuration. It now presses every value of every axis from every layable
starting point — 6,156 control presses — and asserts three things each time:
the pressed axis stands, the result is a floor we lay, and every axis that
actually moved is named in the narration. That is what found it.

Also: /whats-new stopped at 2026-08-22, before the largest visible change to
this site in a month. There is a 'tool' kind and five entries using it for
exactly this sort of change. The new entry says what the page does now and
names the fumed-maple refusal as the example, because the refusal is the part
that makes the company look like it knows what it is doing, which it does.

65 of 66 guards here; verify:css needs an install. parse-scan clean on 642
files. verify:changelog passes at 29 entries.

